(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-physiology-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form B","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How do peroxisomes differ from lysosomes?","choices":["Peroxisomes only exist within skeletal muscle cells, entirely absent from every other tissue type","Peroxisomes are formed by self-replication and contain oxidases (rather than acid hydrolases) to oxidize potentially poisonous substances like alcohol","Peroxisomes are considerably larger than the lysosomes and contain their own circular mitochondrial-like DNA","Peroxisomes have no surrounding membrane at all, existing as free enzyme clusters within the cytosol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Peroxisomes are physically similar to lysosomes but differ in two ways: they are formed by self-replication and contain oxidases rather than acid hydrolases, functioning to oxidize substances like alcohol that might otherwise be poisonous. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the function of autophagy?","choices":["A process for replicating nuclear DNA before mitosis, ensuring accurate duplication of every chromosome","A housekeeping process for degrading and recycling obsolete organelles and large protein aggregates, important for tissue development and cell survival when nutrients are scarce","A process for synthesizing entirely new proteins directly at the rough endoplasmic reticulum's ribosomes","A process for actively transporting ions across the plasma membrane against their own concentration gradient, using ATP directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Autophagy is a housekeeping process that degrades and recycles obsolete organelles and large protein aggregates, important for tissue development and cell survival during nutrient scarcity. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the primary function of mitochondria?","choices":["Extraction of energy from nutrients","Long-term storage of nuclear DNA","Synthesis of membrane phospholipids","Synthesis of new cytoplasmic proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"The primary function of mitochondria is the extraction of energy from nutrients (ATP production). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which cytoskeletal element is composed of heterodimers of alpha and beta tubulin, making up spindle fibers and the core of the axoneme structure?","choices":["Microtubules","Thin filaments (F-actin)","Thick filaments (myosin)","Intermediate filaments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Microtubules are heterodimers of alpha and beta tubulin, forming spindle fibers and the core axoneme structure of cilia/flagella; intermediate filaments are cell-specific fibrillar monomers, thin filaments are F-actin, and thick filaments are myosin. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the approximate functional diameter of a nuclear pore, and what is its approximate molecular weight permeability limit?","choices":["100 nm structural diameter, permeable to molecules of unlimited molecular weight","~1 nm functional diameter, impermeable to molecules above 500 MW","~9 nm functional diameter, permeable to molecules up to ~44,000 MW","9 nm structural diameter, permeable to proteins of any molecular weight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nuclear pores are 100 nm in diameter structurally, but their functional diameter is about 9 nm, selectively permeable to molecules up to about 44,000 MW. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the function of the nucleolus?","choices":["It is the primary site of cellular ATP production via oxidative phosphorylation","It stores tightly condensed DNA, in the form of chromatin, exclusively","It regulates the opening and closing of nuclear membrane ion channels","It contains RNA and proteins and functions to form the granular subunits of ribosomes"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nucleolus (one or more per nucleus, not membrane-delimited) contains RNA and proteins and functions to form the granular subunits of ribosomes; chromatin (condensed DNA) is found separately in the nucleoplasm. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Where do molecules concentrate before receptor-mediated endocytosis proceeds?","choices":["Clathrin-coated pits on the cell surface","Cisternae within the Golgi apparatus","The matrix of peroxisomes","The interior of the nucleolus"],"answer":0,"exp":"In receptor-mediated endocytosis, molecules attach to cell-surface receptors concentrated in clathrin-coated pits; receptor binding induces invagination, and the process is ATP-dependent, involving actin and myosin. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"According to the three-step overview of ATP production, what is the maximum number of ATP molecules formed per molecule of glucose degraded?","choices":["38","2","100","10"],"answer":0,"exp":"A maximum of 38 molecules of ATP are formed per molecule of glucose degraded, following the conversion to glucose/amino acids/fatty acids, processing into Acetyl-CoA, and reaction with O2. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What drives forward movement during ameboid locomotion?","choices":["Passive diffusion only, requiring no ATP hydrolysis or cytoskeletal proteins at all","Continual endocytosis at the tail and exocytosis at the leading edge, with forward movement from ATP-dependent interaction of actin and myosin","Osmotic pressure alone, drawing water into the leading edge in order to passively push the cell forward","Ongoing DNA replication occurring continuously within the moving cell's own nucleus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ameboid locomotion involves continual endocytosis at the \"tail\" and exocytosis at the leading edge (pseudopodium), with forward movement resulting from ATP-dependent interaction of actin and myosin. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the key functional difference between motile cilia and non-motile (primary) cilia?","choices":["Motile cilia require no ATP at all, moving instead through passive osmotic pressure changes","Motile cilia produce whiplike movements (e.g., respiratory airways, fallopian tubes); non-motile cilia occur singly per cell and serve as sensory antennae","Non-motile cilia are found only within the skeletal muscle fibres and are entirely absent everywhere else in the body","There is no functional difference at all; both types produce identical whiplike movements constantly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Motile cilia produce whiplike movements mainly on surfaces like the respiratory airways and fallopian tubes (requiring ATP and Ca2+), while non-motile (primary) cilia generally occur as a single cilium per cell and serve as sensory antennae. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What are the three types of RNA involved in protein synthesis discussed in this lecture, and what is the role of mRNA specifically?","choices":["mRNA, tRNA, rRNA — mRNA is complementary to the DNA coding strand and organized into codons that each code for one amino acid","mRNA directly folds itself up into functional polypeptide chains, entirely without any translation step at all","mRNA is identical in sequence to the DNA coding strand, sharing thymine bases instead of uracil","Only tRNA is involved in protein synthesis, with no functional role for mRNA or rRNA"],"answer":0,"exp":"Messenger RNA (mRNA) is complementary in sequence to the DNA coding strand and organized in codons (triplet bases), with each codon coding for one amino acid (except AUG, the start codon specific for methionine). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"How does tRNA recognize which amino acid to deliver during translation?","choices":["By binding directly to the DNA coding strand within the nucleus during transcription","By purely random chance, with no sequence-specific recognition mechanism involved at all","Each tRNA combines with one amino acid and recognizes a specific mRNA codon via a complementary anticodon","tRNA does not interact with mRNA at all, instead binding ribosomal RNA directly for amino acid delivery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each transfer RNA (tRNA) acts as a carrier molecule, combining with one specific amino acid and recognizing a specific codon via a complementary anticodon on the tRNA molecule. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"What is the effect of sphingomyelin in the myelin sheath on membrane capacitance and ion flow?","choices":["It decreases membrane capacitance and ion flow roughly 5000-fold","It only affects ion flow at the node of Ranvier, not elsewhere","It has no effect on capacitance, only slightly slowing ion flow","It increases membrane capacitance and ion flow substantially"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sphingomyelin in the myelin sheath (formed by Schwann cells wrapping the axon) decreases membrane capacitance and ion flow roughly 5000-fold, enabling faster, more energy-efficient saltatory conduction. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Where are Na+ and K+ channels concentrated in a myelinated axon, enabling saltatory conduction?","choices":["Concentrated at the nodes of Ranvier only","Concentrated only at the axon hillock, not elsewhere","Concentrated only at the axon terminal, not elsewhere","Evenly distributed along the entire length of the axon"],"answer":0,"exp":"In myelinated axons, action potentials occur only at the nodes of Ranvier, where Na+ and K+ channels are concentrated — saltatory conduction (\"jumping\" node to node) increases AP velocity and conserves energy. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In a fast chemical synapse, how does a transmitter released from the presynaptic neuron affect the postsynaptic membrane?","choices":["It always causes hyperpolarization, regardless of receptor type","It only acts through slow second-messenger cascades, never directly","It directly opens ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic neuron","It has no direct electrical effect on the postsynaptic membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"In fast synapses, small-molecule transmitter substances released from the presynaptic neuron bind to receptors on the postsynaptic neuron, directly opening ligand-gated ion channels; the selectivity of these channels for particular ions determines whether the membrane depolarizes or hyperpolarizes. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How do graded (electrotonic) potentials differ from action potentials?","choices":["They have a brief refractory period identical to the action potential, during which a second graded potential cannot be generated at exactly the same membrane site","They are all-or-none events that propagate without decrement over long distances along the axon, exactly like the action potential that follows them","They involve no changes in ion permeability at all, arising instead from purely passive capacitive charging of the membrane at the site of stimulation","They are proportional to stimulus strength (graded), decay exponentially with distance, and can summate, unlike the all-or-none, non-summating, non-decremental AP"],"answer":3,"exp":"Graded (electrotonic) potentials are proportional to stimulus strength, are not propagated but instead decay exponentially with distance, exhibit summation, and have no refractory period — in contrast to the all-or-none, non-decremental, non-summating action potential. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the difference between temporal and spatial summation of excitatory postsynaptic potentials (EPSPs)?","choices":["Temporal summation involves successive EPSPs from the same synapse; spatial summation involves EPSPs from distant/different synapses that overlap","Temporal summation only occurs at inhibitory synapses, while spatial summation is a phenomenon unique to excitatory glutamatergic synapses","They are the same phenomenon, both describing successive EPSPs from a single synapse that summate because they arrive faster than the membrane can decay","Spatial summation requires no overlap in timing at all, only requiring that the summating EPSPs originate from anatomically adjacent dendrites"],"answer":0,"exp":"Temporal summation occurs when successive EPSPs from the same synapse summate because a new EPSP arrives before the previous one has fully decayed; spatial summation occurs when EPSPs from distant/different synapses overlap. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which ions are typically involved in generating an inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP)?","choices":["Na+ and Ca2+","Only Ca2+","K+ or Cl-","Only Na+"],"answer":2,"exp":"An IPSP typically results from increased permeability to K+ or Cl-, which hyperpolarizes the postsynaptic cell and depresses excitability (as opposed to the cation-mediated EPSP). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the approximate equilibrium potential of the excitatory nicotinic ACh receptor/glutamate-type synapse, and what does it do to the postsynaptic cell?","choices":["~0 mV; depolarizes the postsynaptic cell (permeable to Na+, K+, and Ca2+), enhancing excitability","~ -90 mV; permeable mainly to Cl- ions, similar to GABA-A and glycine receptor channels, hyperpolarizing the postsynaptic cell and reducing its excitability","~ -70 mV; equal to the normal resting potential, so opening this channel produces no net current and has essentially no effect on excitability","~ +90 mV; permeable only to Ca2+, which always triggers an action potential directly at the postsynaptic membrane without requiring further summation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Excitatory synapses (e.g., nAChR, glutamate) are permeable to cations (Na+, K+, Ca2+) with a net equilibrium potential around 0 mV, depolarizing the postsynaptic cell and enhancing excitability — contrasted with inhibitory synapses (GABAA, glycine) permeable to Cl- with an equilibrium potential around -90 mV. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the motor end plate?","choices":["A specialized region of the presynaptic neuron only, containing the entire vesicle pool of acetylcholine ready for release into the synaptic cleft","A specialized junction structure found only in smooth muscle, where autonomic varicosities release transmitter diffusely across a wide synaptic gap","The site of concentrated Golgi apparatus within the muscle fiber, responsible for packaging newly synthesized membrane proteins near the sarcolemma","The specialized structure on the muscle fiber where the neuromuscular junction (synapse between motoneuron and muscle fiber) occurs, usually one per fiber"],"answer":3,"exp":"The neuromuscular junction is a specialized synapse between a motoneuron and a muscle fiber, occurring at a structure on the muscle fiber called the motor end plate — usually only one per fiber. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is found in the synaptic cleft of the neuromuscular junction in large quantities, and what is its function?","choices":["Acetylcholinesterase (AChE), which terminates the signal by breaking down ACh","Calmodulin, which binds Ca2+ to activate a postsynaptic kinase","Titin, which provides passive elasticity to the postsynaptic membrane","Troponin, which regulates postsynaptic sensitivity to acetylcholine"],"answer":0,"exp":"The synaptic cleft (20-30 nm wide) of the neuromuscular junction contains large quantities of acetylcholinesterase (AChE), which terminates ACh signaling; ACh-gated channels sit at the tops of the subneural clefts, while voltage-gated Na+ channels are in the bottom part. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What triggers the fusion of synaptic vesicles with the presynaptic membrane and release of ACh at the neuromuscular junction?","choices":["Local depolarization opening voltage-gated Ca2+ channels at the nerve terminal, increasing cytosolic Ca2+, which triggers vesicle fusion and exocytosis","A rise in extracellular Na+ concentration alone, which is thought to draw synaptic vesicles toward the presynaptic membrane through a simple osmotic gradient effect","A drop in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration following the action potential, which is thought to relieve a resting inhibition on vesicle docking proteins and permit fusion","Direct binding of ACh to its own presynaptic autoreceptor, which triggers a positive feedback loop that further accelerates vesicle release"],"answer":0,"exp":"The action potential at the nerve terminal causes local depolarization that opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels; the resulting increase in cytosolic Ca2+ triggers fusion of roughly 125 synaptic vesicles with the presynaptic membrane, releasing ACh by exocytosis. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What are miniature end-plate potentials (MEPPs), and do they cause muscle contraction?","choices":["Potentials generated only in smooth muscle tissue, arising from spontaneous pacemaker activity of gap-junction-coupled cells rather than any vesicle fusion event","Full-blown action potentials generated spontaneously in the postsynaptic muscle fiber itself, entirely independent of any presynaptic acetylcholine release event","Large depolarizations caused by hundreds of vesicles fusing simultaneously in an uncontrolled burst; unlike a normal end-plate potential, they do cause contraction on their own","Small potentials caused by occasional spontaneous fusion of single ACh-filled vesicles with the presynaptic membrane; they do NOT cause muscle contraction"],"answer":3,"exp":"MEPPs arise when ACh-filled vesicles occasionally fuse spontaneously with the presynaptic membrane and release their contents, producing miniature end-plate potentials in the postsynaptic membrane — these do not cause muscle contraction on their own (unlike the summed effect of ~125 vesicles released by an action potential). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What ions does the ACh-gated (nicotinic) channel at the neuromuscular junction conduct, and what is its net equilibrium potential?","choices":["Na+, K+, and Ca2+ (a non-selective cation channel), with a net equilibrium potential of about 0 mV","Only Ca2+, functioning as a highly selective channel analogous to a voltage-gated L-type channel, with an equilibrium potential of about +60 mV","Only K+, functioning as a ligand-gated leak channel with an equilibrium potential around -90 mV, dampening rather than exciting the end plate","Only Cl-, functioning as an inhibitory ligand-gated channel with an equilibrium potential around -90 mV, similar to the GABA-A receptor"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ACh-gated channel is a non-selective cation channel that does not differentiate between Na+, K+, or Ca2+; each ion moves down its own electrochemical gradient, giving a net equilibrium potential of about 0 mV. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What are the three major levels of CNS function described in this lecture?","choices":["Peripheral, central, and autonomic levels of nervous system organization","Spinal cord level, lower brain level, and higher brain (cortical) level","Cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem levels of motor coordination","Sensory, motor, and integrative divisions of cortical function"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three major levels of CNS function are the spinal cord level (contains circuits for walking, withdrawal, posture, organ reflexes), the lower brain level (medulla, pons, mesencephalon, hypothalamus, thalamus, cerebellum, basal ganglia — controls subconscious activities), and the higher brain/cortical level. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"According to this lecture, is the spinal cord merely a conduit for signals passing to and from the brain?","choices":["No — the cord contains its own circuits for walking, withdrawal reflexes, support against gravity, and reflex control of organ function","Yes — the cord merely relays signals passively between peripheral nerves and the brain, with no independent reflex circuitry of its own","The spinal cord's circuits are functionally identical to the cortex, generating voluntary movement without any brainstem input","The spinal cord only contains sensory relay neurons, lacking any motor neurons or interneurons capable of reflex activity"],"answer":0,"exp":"The spinal cord is more than just a conduit for signals — it contains its own circuits for walking, withdrawal reflexes, support against gravity, and reflex control of organ function. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What are the three major structural components of a neuron, and which is the \"effector\" portion?","choices":["Dendrite, synapse, soma — the soma is the effector portion (generates the outgoing signal); the synapse is the sensory portion","Soma, nucleus, cytoplasm — the nucleus is the effector portion (extends to the synaptic terminal); the cytoplasm is sensory","Axon, myelin, node of Ranvier — the node of Ranvier is the effector portion; the myelin sheath is the sensory portion","Soma, axon, dendrite — the axon is the effector portion (extends to the synaptic terminal); the dendrite is the sensory portion"],"answer":3,"exp":"A neuron's three major components are the soma (main body), axon (extends to the synaptic terminal, the effector portion), and dendrites (projections from the soma, the sensory portion). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is the chief excitatory transmitter in the CNS, opening cation channels to create an EPSP?","choices":["Serotonin","GABA","Glycine","Glutamate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glutamate is the chief excitatory transmitter in the CNS, opening cation channels (conducting Na+, K+, Ca2+) to create an excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is the chief inhibitory transmitter in the adult CNS, opening Cl- channels to create an IPSP?","choices":["Dopamine (a catecholamine)","Glutamate (an amino acid)","GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid)","Acetylcholine (cholinergic)"],"answer":2,"exp":"GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the chief inhibitory transmitter in the adult CNS, opening Cl- channels to hyperpolarize the postsynaptic cell and create an IPSP (though it can be excitatory during development due to differences in Cl- reversal potential). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Why does excitation of a single presynaptic neuron on a dendrite almost never induce an action potential by itself?","choices":["Dendrites cannot receive any synaptic input directly, since all incoming terminals synapse exclusively onto the axon hillock instead","Single synapses always produce large EPSPs (~10-15 mV), but voltage-gated K+ channels on the dendrite immediately shunt the signal to zero","Dendrites have abundant voltage-gated Na+ channels that actively block transmission of any single subthreshold synaptic input","Each terminal produces only a small EPSP (~0.5-1.0 mV); multiple terminals must be excited simultaneously (spatial summation) for the combined EPSP to exceed threshold"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each synaptic terminal on a dendrite produces only a small EPSP (about 0.5-1.0 mV); an action potential requires spatial summation — multiple terminals excited simultaneously — for the combined EPSP to exceed threshold. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What does it mean for a neuron to be \"facilitated\"?","choices":["The summated postsynaptic potential is nearer the threshold for firing than normal but has not yet reached it, making the neuron easier to stimulate with subsequent input","The neuron is permanently inhibited by continuous GABA release, making it impossible to stimulate with any amount of subsequent input","Facilitation only applies to inhibitory neurons, whose postsynaptic potential moves further from threshold with repeated stimulation","The neuron has already fired an action potential and remains in its absolute refractory period, unable to respond to further input"],"answer":0,"exp":"A facilitated neuron has a summated postsynaptic potential nearer the threshold for firing than normal (but not yet at threshold), making it easier to stimulate to firing with subsequent input. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Do dendrites typically transmit action potentials?","choices":["No — dendrites have few voltage-gated Na+ channels and instead transmit signals by electrotonic conduction","Dendrites only conduct true action potentials during periods of maximal spatial summation and high-frequency input","Dendrites and axons are functionally identical, both propagating full-amplitude action potentials via saltatory conduction","Yes, dendrites have abundant voltage-gated Na+ channels, propagating action potentials just as effectively as the axon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Dendrites do not typically transmit action potentials because they have few voltage-gated Na+ channels; instead, they transmit signals by electrotonic conduction, allowing summation of signals from many presynaptic neurons over a large spatial area. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of synaptic facilitation (enhanced responsiveness following repetitive stimulation)?","choices":["Decreased calcium influx at the presynaptic terminal, reducing vesicular release with each successive stimulus","Downregulation of postsynaptic receptors following repeated exposure to the same neurotransmitter over seconds","Progressive depletion of the readily releasable pool of neurotransmitter vesicles at the presynaptic terminal","Build-up of calcium ions in presynaptic terminals, causing more vesicular release of transmitter"],"answer":3,"exp":"Synaptic facilitation is enhanced responsiveness following repetitive stimulation, caused by a build-up of calcium ions in the presynaptic terminal, which increases vesicular release of transmitter. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the functional significance of synaptic fatigue (short-term synaptic depression)?","choices":["It permanently destroys the presynaptic terminal, contributing to the progression of neurodegenerative disease","It only occurs in the peripheral nervous system, playing no role in central circuits like those involved in seizures","It is a protective mechanism against excessive neuronal activity, and may be a mechanism by which epileptic seizures end","It has no protective function, instead worsening excitotoxic damage during periods of sustained neuronal firing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Synaptic fatigue occurs when excitatory synapses are stimulated repetitively at a rapid rate until postsynaptic discharge progressively decreases — a protective mechanism against excessive neuronal activity, possibly contributing to the end of epileptic seizures. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What are the two main categories of neurotransmitters described, and how do they differ in speed of action?","choices":["Small molecule, rapidly acting transmitters and neuropeptides, slower acting transmitters","Ionotropic transmitters and metabotropic transmitters, distinguished by receptor type rather than speed","Only small molecule transmitters exist in the CNS, since neuropeptides are restricted to the periphery","Fast-acting amines and slow-acting steroids, distinguished by their site of synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Neurotransmitters fall into two main categories: small molecule, rapidly acting transmitters (mediate most acute nervous system responses) and neuropeptides, slower acting transmitters that cause longer-term changes. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How is glutamate synthesized and released, in contrast to vesicular transmitters?","choices":["It is stored long-term in dense-core vesicles, released only by calcium-triggered exocytosis","It requires no synthesis at all, existing in unlimited free supply within the extracellular space","It is only released via exocytosis of pre-formed vesicles stored at the presynaptic terminal","It is synthesized on demand and does not use vesicles — it diffuses through the membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glutamate (the chief excitatory CNS transmitter, accounting for over 90% of CNS synaptic connections) is synthesized on demand and does not use vesicles for release — it diffuses through the membrane. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the neural circuit basis of the flexor withdrawal reflex?","choices":["Nociceptor activation transmitted to the cord synapses with interneurons that diverge to withdrawal muscles, inhibit antagonists, and activate reverberating circuits to prolong contraction","It only involves gamma motor neurons, adjusting muscle spindle sensitivity in the withdrawing limb rather than producing actual limb movement","It is a purely monosynaptic reflex with no interneurons at all, in which the nociceptor afferent synapses directly onto the alpha-motoneuron","It requires cortical input for every single occurrence, since the isolated spinal cord alone cannot generate a coordinated withdrawal response"],"answer":0,"exp":"The flexor withdrawal reflex begins with nociceptor activation transmitted to the spinal cord, which synapses with interneurons that diverge to stimulate withdrawal muscles, inhibit antagonist muscles, and activate reverberating circuits that prolong the muscle contraction (after-discharge). — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the function of the crossed-extensor reflex, and when does it begin relative to the flexor withdrawal reflex?","choices":["It has no consistent timing relationship to the flexor withdrawal reflex, occurring at random intervals from seconds to minutes after the painful stimulus","It occurs simultaneously with the flexor reflex in the very same limb, reinforcing rather than opposing the ongoing withdrawal movement","It elicits an extensor reflex in the opposite limb beginning 0.2-0.5 seconds after the painful stimulus (after the flexor reflex), pushing the body away and shifting weight to the opposite limb","It only occurs within the same limb as the painful stimulus, extending that limb further rather than shifting weight to the opposite side"],"answer":2,"exp":"The crossed-extensor reflex elicits an extensor reflex in the limb opposite to a painful stimulus, beginning 0.2-0.5 seconds after the stimulus (after the flexor withdrawal reflex) — this serves to push the body away from the stimulus and shift weight to the opposite limb. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How does the abundance of interneurons compare to anterior motor neurons, and what role do they play?","choices":["Interneurons and motor neurons exist in roughly equal numbers, with each anterior motor neuron paired one-to-one with its own dedicated interneuron","Interneurons are far less abundant than motor neurons, are large and poorly excitable, and play essentially no role in mediating spinal reflexes","Interneurons are about 30x more abundant than anterior motor neurons, are small and highly excitable, and comprise most of the neural circuitry for motor reflexes","Most signals descending from the brain bypass interneurons entirely, synapsing directly and exclusively onto anterior motor neurons themselves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Interneurons are about 30 times more abundant than anterior motor neurons, are small and highly excitable, and most signals from the brain terminate on interneurons, which comprise the neural circuitry for motor reflexes. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the function of propriospinal fibers?","choices":["They travel up and down the cord for 1-2 segments, providing pathways for multisegmental reflexes and contributing to proprioception","They exist only within the cervical enlargement of the spinal cord, coordinating reflexes exclusively between the arms and neck","They carry signals only from the brain down to the cord, having no intraspinal function or role in multisegmental reflex pathways","They exit the spinal cord entirely to innervate the skin, functioning as purely cutaneous sensory fibers rather than intraspinal ones"],"answer":0,"exp":"Propriospinal fibers travel up and down the spinal cord for 1-2 segments, providing pathways for multisegmental reflexes and playing an important role in proprioception. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why do muscles like the extraocular muscles and hand/neck muscles have a higher density of muscle spindles than muscles like the quadriceps?","choices":["Spindle density is entirely unrelated to the precision of movement a muscle performs, depending instead purely on its total cross-sectional area and bulk","Larger muscles always have proportionally more spindles than smaller ones, regardless of whether the movements they perform are coarse or fine","Larger muscles performing coarse movements need fewer spindles, while smaller muscles performing fine movements need many spindles for precise control","All muscles have identical spindle density regardless of size, since spindle number is fixed genetically rather than functionally determined"],"answer":2,"exp":"Muscle spindle density varies according to function: larger muscles with coarse movements (e.g., quadriceps) have relatively few spindles, while smaller muscles performing fine movements (e.g., extraocular muscles, hand and neck muscles) have many spindles for precise control. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What happens to muscle contraction smoothness when the sensory afferents from a muscle spindle are severed?","choices":["Muscle contraction becomes noticeably stronger and faster once severed, since the spindle normally exerts a braking effect that is removed along with it","Contraction remains perfectly smooth, since the smoothing function is actually provided by the Golgi tendon organ rather than the muscle spindle","The muscle can no longer contract at all, since alpha motor neuron output is thought to depend entirely on an intact afferent signal from the spindle","Muscle contraction becomes jerky, since the stretch reflex's smoothing function (via the muscle spindle) is lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"The stretch reflex normally smooths out muscle contractions; when sensory afferents from a muscle spindle are sectioned, muscle contraction becomes noticeably jerky, demonstrating the muscle spindle system's smoothing function. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"In amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), which motor neurons deteriorate, and which are relatively spared?","choices":["Only gamma motor neurons deteriorate in ALS, sparing both the upper and lower alpha motor neurons throughout the entire course of the disease","Upper and lower alpha motor neurons deteriorate; gamma motor neurons are relatively spared","All motor neuron types, including alpha, gamma, and spinal interneurons, are affected equally and simultaneously right from disease onset","Interneurons deteriorate exclusively in ALS, while both alpha and gamma motor neurons remain completely functional throughout the disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"In ALS (Lou Gehrig's disease), upper and lower alpha motor neurons deteriorate while gamma motor neurons are relatively spared — the gamma neurons may even contribute to disease progression by increasing muscle stiffness and spasticity via muscle spindle activation. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Does the Golgi tendon organ only signal muscle force at very high tension levels?","choices":["It has no functional signaling range at all, remaining completely silent until the muscle-tendon unit is on the verge of mechanical failure","It only responds to changes in muscle length, not tension, functioning as a redundant backup sensor duplicating the muscle spindle's role","Yes -- it only activates near the point of muscle tear, remaining completely silent throughout the normal physiological range of tension","No — it signals muscle force through the entire physiological range of tension, not just at high levels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Golgi tendon organs signal muscle force through the entire physiological range of tension (functioning to equalize force among muscle fibers), not only at the very high tension levels associated with the protective autogenic inhibition reflex. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Does activation of a Golgi tendon organ in one muscle affect adjacent muscles?","choices":["Yes — Golgi tendon organ activation is relayed through spinal interneurons to inhibit alpha motor neurons in every synergist of that limb","No — the Golgi tendon organ reflex only affects the individual muscle it is located in; adjacent muscles are not affected","Only in part — it inhibits its own agonist while simultaneously facilitating the antagonist muscle across the same joint","Yes, but only muscles sharing the same spinal segment are affected, via a fast monosynaptic reflex arc"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Golgi tendon organ reflex only affects an individual muscle — adjacent muscles are not affected by activation of a given Golgi tendon organ. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"At approximately what velocity is muscle length/tension information transmitted from muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs to higher brain centers?","choices":["6 m/sec","120 m/sec","1000 m/sec","0.5 m/sec"],"answer":1,"exp":"Signals from muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs are transmitted to higher centers at about 120 m/sec, informing the brain of instantaneous changes in muscle tension and length for feedback control of motor activity. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Where do the basic circuits for walking reflexes reside?","choices":["Only in the cerebral cortex","Only in the cerebellum","Only in the basal ganglia","In the spinal cord"],"answer":3,"exp":"Basic walking reflexes reside in the spinal cord itself, illustrating that the cord contains neuronal circuits for locomotion beyond simply conducting signals to and from higher centers. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is Westphal's sign, and what can cause it?","choices":["A pathologically exaggerated (hyperactive) patellar reflex caused specifically by cerebellar disease, without any accompanying muscle weakness","Absence or decrease of the patellar reflex, which can be caused by receptor damage, peripheral nerve disease, or lesions in the motor cortex/pyramidal tracts","A pathologically brisk, clonus-producing patellar reflex resulting from loss of descending inhibitory input from the cortex onto the spinal reflex arc","Hyperactivity of gamma motor neurons causing increased muscle spindle sensitivity and an exaggerated, brisk patellar reflex response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Westphal's sign is the absence or decrease of the patellar reflex, which can result from many causes including receptor damage, peripheral nerve disease, or a lesion in the motor cortex or pyramidal tracts. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is autonomic \"tone,\" and what is its functional significance?","choices":["The maximal possible activity of a system, a fixed ceiling reached only during mass discharge that cannot be exceeded regardless of further sympathetic or parasympathetic stimulation","The basal rate of activity present only in the parasympathetic system, since the sympathetic system has no baseline activity and only fires during acute stress","The basal (background) rate of activity of the sympathetic or parasympathetic system, which allows activity to increase or decrease from that baseline (e.g., sympathetic tone causes about 50% vasoconstriction at baseline)","A historical term with no real physiological basis, since neither division maintains any baseline activity between discrete reflex responses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Autonomic tone is the basal rate of activity of each system; this background activity allows for an increase OR decrease in activity by a single system — sympathetic tone normally causes about 50% vasoconstriction, and parasympathetic tone provides background GI activity. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What triggers mass discharge of the sympathetic nervous system (the \"fight-or-flight\" reaction), and what happens to blood flow to the GI tract?","choices":["It is triggered by parasympathetic activation during rest and digestion; blood flow to the GI tract increases while flow to skeletal muscle decreases correspondingly","Activation of the hypothalamus by fright or severe pain; blood flow to the GI tract decreases while flow to active muscles increases","It is triggered only by exposure to cold temperatures, activating brown fat thermogenesis while leaving GI and muscle blood flow essentially unchanged","Mass discharge has no effect on GI blood flow, redirecting cardiac output solely toward the skin and kidneys instead of active skeletal muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Mass discharge of the SNS occurs when the hypothalamus is activated by fright or severe pain (the fight-or-flight reaction), causing increased blood flow to active muscles and decreased blood flow to the GI tract and kidneys, along with increased heart rate, metabolism, blood glucose, and muscle strength. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of cholinesterase inhibitor drugs like neostigmine and pyridostigmine?","choices":["They deplete presynaptic acetylcholine stores by blocking its synthesis","They potentiate the effect of acetylcholine by inhibiting its breakdown","They stimulate nicotinic receptors directly, mimicking acetylcholine itself","They block acetylcholine receptors directly, preventing its binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cholinesterase inhibitors (neostigmine, pyridostigmine, ambenonium) potentiate the effect of acetylcholine by inhibiting the enzyme that normally breaks it down, prolonging its action at the synapse. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of antimuscarinic drugs like atropine and scopolamine?","choices":["They selectively block nicotinic receptors at autonomic ganglia rather than muscarinic receptors on effector cells","They increase acetylcholine synthesis and release from postganglionic parasympathetic terminals","They stimulate muscarinic receptors, mimicking and enhancing the effect of acetylcholine on effector cells","They block the effect of acetylcholine on effector cells (at muscarinic receptors)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Antimuscarinic drugs such as atropine and scopolamine block the effect of acetylcholine on effector cells at muscarinic receptors. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of ganglionic blockers like hexamethonium?","choices":["They enhance transmission between pre- and postganglionic neurons by prolonging nicotinic receptor activation at the ganglionic synapse","They block transmission at the autonomic ganglia (between pre- and postganglionic neurons)","They selectively block beta-1 receptors on the heart, without affecting transmission at the autonomic ganglia themselves","They increase norepinephrine release from postganglionic terminals by promoting vesicular exocytosis at the neuroeffector junction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ganglionic blockers like hexamethonium block transmission at the autonomic ganglia, between the pre- and postganglionic neurons, affecting both sympathetic and parasympathetic pathways. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What are the respective effects of sympathetic and parasympathetic stimulation on the eye?","choices":["Neither division has any effect on pupil diameter, which is controlled entirely by ambient light reflexes at the retina","Sympathetic causes pupillary dilation; parasympathetic causes pupillary constriction and lens accommodation for near vision","Both divisions cause pupillary dilation, differing only in the speed and duration of the dilation response produced","Sympathetic causes lens accommodation for near vision; parasympathetic causes pupillary dilation for distance vision"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation causes pupillary dilation, while parasympathetic stimulation causes pupillary constriction and accommodation (focusing) of the lens for near vision. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which autonomic division stimulates the nasal, lacrimal, salivary, and GI glands, and which stimulates sweat glands?","choices":["Both divisions stimulate all gland types equally","Parasympathetic stimulates nasal/lacrimal/salivary/GI glands; sympathetic stimulates sweat glands","Neither division affects glandular secretion","Sympathetic stimulates nasal/lacrimal/salivary/GI glands; parasympathetic stimulates sweat glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parasympathetic stimulation activates the nasal, lacrimal, salivary, and GI glands, while sympathetic stimulation activates sweat glands — an exception to the general pattern since sweat glands are sympathetically (cholinergically) innervated. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What are the respective effects of sympathetic and parasympathetic stimulation on heart rate and blood vessels?","choices":["Both divisions produce identical increases in heart rate and identical peripheral vasoconstriction, differing only in which cranial or spinal segments carry the outgoing signal to the heart","Sympathetic increases heart rate and causes vasoconstriction; parasympathetic decreases heart rate and causes some vasodilation","Parasympathetic stimulation has no measurable effect on heart rate or blood vessels, acting instead exclusively on glandular secretion and pupil size","Sympathetic stimulation decreases heart rate and causes vasodilation; parasympathetic stimulation causes vasoconstriction with little effect on rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate and contractility and causes vasoconstriction, while parasympathetic stimulation decreases heart rate and causes some vasodilation; sympathetic stimulation has very little direct effect on GI activity, which is primarily driven by parasympathetic stimulation. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"From what amino acid are norepinephrine and epinephrine synthesized?","choices":["Glutamate (via GABA and succinate semialdehyde intermediates)","Tyrosine (via DOPA and dopamine intermediates)","Tryptophan (via 5-hydroxytryptophan and serotonin intermediates)","Glycine (via serine and threonine intermediates)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Norepinephrine and epinephrine are synthesized from the amino acid tyrosine, via the pathway tyrosine → DOPA → dopamine → norepinephrine → (epinephrine). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"From what two components is acetylcholine formed?","choices":["Glutamate and acetyl-CoA","Tyrosine and choline","GABA and choline","Acetyl-CoA and choline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Acetylcholine is formed from a combination of acetyl-CoA and choline. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT among the visceral functions the ANS helps control?","choices":["Voluntary skeletal muscle contraction for walking","GI motility, secretions, and enzyme release throughout the tract","Arterial pressure regulation and heart rate control","Urinary bladder emptying and sweat gland secretion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ANS controls most visceral functions (arterial pressure, heart rate/contractility, GI motility/secretions, bladder emptying, sweating, body temperature, vascular tone, airways), but voluntary skeletal muscle contraction (e.g., for walking) is controlled by the somatic motor system, not the ANS. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What poison activates muscarinic receptors, giving them their name?","choices":["Muscarine, a toadstool poison","Tetrodotoxin, a pufferfish toxin","Batrachotoxin, a poison dart frog toxin","Nicotine, a tobacco plant alkaloid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Muscarinic receptors are named for their activation by muscarine, a toadstool poison, while nicotinic receptors are named for their activation by nicotine. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 12"}]};
